course: medicine
lesson: 26
title: Emergencies: ambulance, 999 and A&E
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: When someone's life may be in danger, someone not breathing, unconscious, badly bleeding or with a bad fall, you call 999 in the UK, and you will never be in trouble for calling in a real emergency.
explain: The call handler is trained to help even a frightened child: they ask where you are and what happened, send help, and tell you what to do until it arrives, so you don't hang up.
explain: The most important words you can say are your address, which is why learning it by heart is a real safety skill.
explain: Not every problem needs 999: for urgent but not life-threatening worries, like a high fever at night, UK families ring 111, and small injuries can go to an urgent treatment centre.
fact: 999 is the UK emergency number for ambulance, fire, police or coastguard, and 112 also works in the UK and across Europe.
fact: The USA and Canada use 911, Australia uses 000 and New Zealand uses 111.
fact: In the UK 111 is the NHS line for urgent medical advice that isn't life-threatening, while in New Zealand 111 is the emergency number.
fact: Ambulances carry paramedics, who are trained to give emergency treatment on the spot and on the way to hospital.
fact: CPR, cardiopulmonary resuscitation, means pressing hard and fast on the middle of the chest to keep blood moving when the heart has stopped, and the call handler can talk anyone through it.
fact: A defibrillator gives an electric shock to restart a heart with a dangerous rhythm; public ones, often in a box marked AED, give spoken instructions and only shock if the heart needs it; unlike in films, the person often needs more CPR and hospital care, and it doesn't always work.
fact: A&E, Accident and Emergency, is for serious injuries and illnesses, and in the USA it is called the ER.
fact: Urgent treatment centres and minor injury units deal with things like sprains, small cuts and minor burns, keeping A&E free for the most serious cases.
fact: In an emergency the most important thing is to tell the services where you are, so children should learn their full address and postcode.
fact: An emergency call can be made from a locked mobile phone.
fact: The recovery position, on the side with the top leg bent and the head tilted back, keeps the airway open in someone who is unconscious but breathing.
fact: Hoax 999 calls take time away from real emergencies and can lead to trouble with the police.
exercise: What number do you call in the UK if Grandad falls and won't wake up? | 999 | 111 | 101 | 911
exercise: Which other number also works for emergencies in the UK and Europe? | 112 | 123 | 000 | 411
exercise: What is the emergency number in the USA? | 911 | 999 | 000 | 111
exercise: What is the emergency number in Australia? | 000 | 911 | 999 | 112
exercise: When would you ring 111 in the UK? | For urgent advice when it isn't life-threatening | When someone has stopped breathing | To report a fire | To order a taxi
exercise: What does 111 mean in New Zealand? | The emergency number | The NHS-style advice line | The weather forecast line | The speaking clock for the time
exercise: Who comes in an ambulance and treats patients on the way? | Paramedics | Pharmacists | Dentists | Teachers
exercise: What is CPR? | Pressing hard and fast on the chest when the heart has stopped | Giving water to a fainted person | Rubbing someone's back | Putting someone to sleep
exercise: Do you need to be a doctor to do CPR? | No, the call handler can talk you through it | Yes, it's only for surgeons in a proper hospital | Yes, or you go to prison | Only paramedics may try
exercise: What does a defibrillator do? | Shocks a heart with a dangerous rhythm to restart it | Measures temperature | Pumps air into the lungs | Takes an X-ray
exercise: Can you harm someone by using a public defibrillator? | No, it only shocks if the heart needs it | Yes, it shocks anyone at once, even if they're fine | Yes, unless you're a doctor | Only on Sundays
exercise: What is A&E called in the USA? | The ER | The GP | The ICU | The OR
exercise: Where might a sprained ankle go instead of A&E? | An urgent treatment centre | The dentist round the corner | The back room of the pharmacy | The school office, with an ice pack
exercise: What's the most important thing to tell the 999 call handler? | Where you are | What you had for lunch | Your favourite colour | Your teacher's name
exercise: Can you call 999 from a locked phone? | Yes | No, you need the passcode | Only with credit | Only from a landline
exercise: What does the recovery position do? | Keeps the airway open in someone unconscious but breathing | Wakes the person up | Stops bleeding | Cures a broken leg
exercise: Why is a prank 999 call harmful? | It takes time from real emergencies | It's free, so it doesn't matter at all | It helps train call handlers | It's harmless fun
test: Friend's on his back, not waking, but breathing. What do you do after calling 999? | Roll him onto his side in the recovery position | Leave him on his back | Sit him up and give him water | Shake him hard
test: "if I call 999 and it's not that bad I'll be arrested" — true? | No, you won't be in trouble for a real emergency | Yes, always | Yes, unless a grown-up makes the call for you instead | Only if you're under 10
test: Holiday in Sydney, someone collapses. Which number? | 000 | 999 | 911 | 111
test: Mum's kid has 39° fever at 2am but is awake and talking. Who's best to ring first in England? | 111 | 999 | The school | 911
test: What would happen if lots of people went to A&E for small splinters? | Seriously ill patients would wait longer | A&E would close for the day | Splinters would be treated faster than heart attacks | Nothing changes
test: In a film the hero zaps someone with a defib and they sit up chatting at once. What's real? | Often it takes more CPR and hospital care, and it doesn't always work | Defibs always wake people instantly | Defibs are only for films | Defibs make people stronger
test: Grandma lives with you. You've never learned your address. Why does it matter? | The call handler needs it to send help | It doesn't, they always know | Only for birthday cards | The ambulance finds you by following its siren
test: Kiwi cousin says "dial 111 for emergency". You're in Leeds. Which is right in Leeds? | 999 for emergencies, 111 for advice | 111 for emergencies, the same as in NZ | 911 in Leeds, just like in American films | 000 everywhere in the world, like Australia
test: Crowd gathers around an ambulance in the street. What should you do? | Move aside and let them work | Go closer to film it | Ask the paramedics questions | Block the road to help
test: Mate dials 999 for a laugh. Best reply? | Stop, it takes help away from people in danger | Do it again, it's funny and nobody ever finds out | It's free, so fine | Only bad if you swear
test: Which needs 999 rather than 111? | Someone not breathing | A cough for three days | A mild sprain | A small graze
test: You're doing CPR at the station and see a box marked AED. What is it? | A defibrillator to use with CPR | A fire alarm for the whole station | A first-aid box full of snacks and drinks | A phone charger for travellers to use
exam: Pursuant to the arrangements in force in the United Kingdom, identify the telephone number to be employed where a life is in immediate danger. | 999 | 111 | 101 | 000
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the NHS 111 service. | It furnishes urgent medical advice in cases not threatening to life | It is reserved for cardiac arrest | It summons the fire service exclusively | It is the emergency number of the United States
exam: Ascertain the purpose of placing an unconscious but breathing person in the recovery position. | The maintenance of an open airway | The prompt restoration of consciousness | The arrest of haemorrhage | The setting of fractures
exam: Whereas a public defibrillator is applied to a person in collapse, determine the conduct of the device. | It administers a shock only where the cardiac rhythm requires it | It administers a shock in every instance | It administers medicament intravenously | It administers oxygen through the nose
